## Hard rules (the anti-confusion controls)
1. **Absolute paths only.** Never say or pass "the repo" — name it with its full path.
2. **Write ONLY here.** A PreToolUse guard (`.claude/hooks/guard-cross-repo-writes.mjs`) blocks
   Write/Edit outside this repo, so a sibling is **physically unwritable** by the file tools.
   (Sanctioned exception: the guard also allows `~/.claude`, `/tmp`, `/private/tmp`, and every
   absolute path in `.claude/kit.config.json` `worktreeRoots` — which is how this repo's private
   worktrees stay writable. A root outside ALL of those is one your file tools cannot reach.)
3. **Identity ritual before EVERY commit/push:** run
   `git -C {{REPO_ROOT_PATH}} remote get-url origin && git -C {{REPO_ROOT_PATH}} branch --show-current`
   and confirm the intended remote + branch. **Pushing `{{DEPLOY_BRANCH}}` {{DEPLOY_EFFECT}}** needs a
   fresh Owner GO for the exact head and target, per the method.
4. **Declare the task lane before the first code write.** `.claude/task-lane.json` binds this session
   id + a kebab task id + exactly one route: **`in-thread`** (with the tier) or **`exempt`** (with a
   ledgered reason **and the tier** — the reason names the unavailable seat, which says nothing about
   how risky the work is, and an exemption never skips a gate). Undeclared / malformed / stale ⇒ the
   hook BLOCKS (fail-closed). The retired `lane` route is REFUSED by both controls (kit v1.5.0).
5. **Multi-writer checkout — surgical staging only.** If more than one agent lane writes this
   checkout, **NEVER `git add -A` / `git add .` / `git commit -a`.** Unexplained dirty files are the
   other lane's in-flight work — never stage, stash, revert, or edit them. **Canonical text for ALL
   lanes: `core/MULTI_AGENT.md` § Multi-writer checkout** — read it before any commit. Claude-specific:
   worktrees go under a root declared in `.claude/kit.config.json` `worktreeRoots` (`/tmp` by
   default — the rule-2 exception); commit early and often there — a scratch root is purged on
   reboot and only commits survive it.

## Enforcement is asymmetric — say it plainly
On Write, Edit, MultiEdit and NotebookEdit, `.claude/settings.json` registers **three fail-closed guards** — cross-repo writes,
lane authoring, brief rung — and **three sensors** (sweep-owed, mutation-owed, context-pressure)
that print and never deny; on Bash **one more sensor**, the gate ladder, which surfaces the tier's
ladder and never denies; on a Claude cross-session send, the brief-rung guard's SEND half, which on a
`SendMessage` to the optional `pairedPmClaudeTarget` (stable ref/id) or `pairedPmClaudeName` owes
the Architect decision screen; and on Stop
**two sensors** (Owner-comms, token ledger) that fail open. The Codex lane's generated
`.codex/hooks.json` registers the three guards and first two sensors on `apply_patch`, the gate
ladder on Bash, and brief-rung on the exact `mcp__codex_app__send_message_to_thread` tool. That
send matcher covers the optional `pairedPmThreadId` in this checkout's `.claude/kit.config.json`;
without a configured pair, ordinary Codex sends are outside this Architect-pair scope. A mixed
Claude/Codex pair has no shared send tool and runs file-only.
Context-pressure and both Stop sensors remain Claude-only. **In the Codex lane these hooks are
INERT until a human grants hook trust interactively; an untrusted hook is skipped SILENTLY.**
`node scripts/check-codex-hooks-armed.mjs` probes `apply_patch` only. The paired send needs
separate actual real-tool Source, Command and Trust proof in this task. Write guards bind tool
calls, so a shell command that rewrites a file passes them in either lane; a human with an editor
loads none of them. What binds **every** lane unconditionally is prose (`AGENTS.md` + the required
PM-disposition emission) **plus the `.githooks/pre-commit` hook** `init` installed
(`core.hooksPath=.githooks`). See the kit's `PORTABILITY.md` (workflow-kit repository). Do not
claim the paired send is armed from the write probe alone.

